00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.070 align:start position:0%
 
why is the rum  always gone  oh that's why  captain jack sparrow of the pirates of  the caribbean movies was known to enjoy  a few sips  of rum and he's not the only one today  the spirit accounts for  2.3 billion dollars in revenue here in  the united states  captain morgan and bacardi are two of  the five best-selling spirit brands of  any kind  recently sales of value rums are down  but  super premium rums are surging one of  those the real mccoy  borrows from the past to separate itself  from that  stiff competition  most people have never really had an  opportunity to taste  completely unadulterated rums as the  founder and ceo of real mccoy rum  bailey pryor is trying to change that  you have all the same traditions and  techniques that you would have in the  finest scotch whiskies the finest cognac  so i can sip rum on the rocks yup neat  on the rocks it's beautiful we can do a  little tasting if you'd like without a  doubt  the mystic connecticut based prior has a  small test distillery  where he can develop new products or  expressions he poured us three now next  we're going to go to the five year  you're going to start getting more of  the barrel in this now you're going to  start to get the lignans and tannins  from the wood so go ahead and give that  a little sip  i'm really amazed at how smooth these  are  that's what people tell me all the time  they say i had no idea rum could taste  like  i had no idea rum could taste like this  prior had  no experience making rum when he created  the real mccoy  he spent decades working in film first  as a production assistant on movies like  mystic pizza and home alone  then making documentaries i do all my  own research and i write all my own  scripts i direct my documentary films  and so  i just get fascinated with subjects and  then i'll go all the way down the rabbit  hole  never more so than with his film about  the legendary rum runner  bill mccoy he's really the only name you  know from the prohibition era who wasn't  a psychopathic murderer  right there's al capone baby face nelson  a machine gun kelly and  the guy who refused to break the law  bill mccoy or rip off his customers  like the world around him bill mccoy's  life was about to turn  upside down pryor's 2012 film  explored how the former shipbuilder  helped create the illicit alcohol trade  up and down the east coast after  ratification of the 18th amendment  he was the first guy to fill up a boat  full of rum in january of 1920 down in  the caribbean he sailed it up to new  york city and acted as a floating liquor  store  three miles offshore a loophole in u.s  law  made that international waters allowing  mccoy to supply  smaller boats which would bring his rum  back to shore  the government had good reason to  silence his legend  he became the face of rum row other  people would cut their alcohols with  turpentine wood alcohol prune juice  water mccoy never did that so they  called his product the real mccoy  and i just thought you know that phrase  it's such a household name i wonder if  anyone's ever trademarked it  turns out no one had so pryor did  then using photographs he traced mccoy's  rum through the tax stamps on its  barrels  to barbados barbados is the birthplace  of rum  so the tradition of rum producing goes  back about 400 years in barbados  pryor and his wife jennifer headed down  to the caribbean island in search of the  distillery that produced mccoy's barrels  they ended up at four square distillery  home to fourth generation rum maker  richard seal so we don't know if that's  where it came from or not  but i asked richard if he would be  willing to work with us and i said i  wanted the kind of rum that would have  been on mccoy's ship  back in 1920 so when someone takes a sip  of this they can taste what tradition is  all about  taste the real mccoy they can taste the  real mccoy and he said well that's all  we make  and that's what started the whole thing  one of the last family-owned  distilleries on barbados  foursquare combines the past with the  present in brewing  the key component to rum sugarcane is  grown on the property  it's turned into molasses and distilled  in a traditional pot still  which slows down the process but adds  more flavor to the rum  everything is done in a sustainable  process as you can possibly do on an  island we're 100 produced in barbados so  we even do the bottling down there  please tell me you are not going three  miles out in the water to pick up the  shipments as they come in the same way  we've talked about having a party out  there pryor says they're now selling up  to 18 000  cases of rum a year after just 1500 in  year one  2013.  he travels to rum festivals around the  world  educating people on how rum should taste  and pryor is so confident that his rum  really is the  real mccoy he lobbied the u.s government  to become the first spirit company to  list its ingredients  on the label i'm convinced that people  today  want to be informed they want  transparency people want to drink less  typically now but they want to drink  better and bill mccoy became an iconic  moral barometer  as complex and controversial as the era  itself  to think back this documentary sort of  started  all of this would you ever have imagined  at the beginning of that that this is  where you'd be  no uh in fact it was sort of a lark you  know we're like hey this is fun we'll  just do this on the side you know  sell to our friends or something like  that and and then it just kind of took  off  i lift a glass to you and say cheers  cheers just thank you jesus  cheers cheers  so as promised  you guys can taste this is the 12. i'm  telling you all three to me we're so  smooth in comparison to other spirits  that i've tasted  you've got the 12 to five and i just  love the smell of it  it's just have a sip just have a so  aromatic there's there's something just  sugary syrupy goodness that you it's  molasses you're right it is it's good  it's very smooth it's very nice not what  you expect from rum  no not always especially when it's aged  12 years i mean that's like  it's delicious

